Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF)
Respiratory System
Progressive dyspnea, Velcro-like inspiratory crackles, Honeycombing on HRCT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-023
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe worsening of baseline shortness of breath and an agonizing dry cough, requiring urgent symptom management and assessment for secondary complications.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with known pulmonary fibrosis experiencing severe, unrelenting paroxysms of coughing and worsening dyspnea at rest, causing profound distress. Urgent assessment was clinically mandated to rule out spontaneous pneumothorax or acute cor pulmonale and to provide potent palliative symptom relief prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't stop this dry, hacking cough, and it feels like I'm suffocating even when I'm just sitting perfectly still."
History of Present Illness: The patient, an older adult, reports a long history of gradually progressive, severe exertional dyspnea. Over the last 48 hours, their chronic, persistent dry cough has become unrelenting, causing chest wall pain and severe fatigue. They report an inability to catch their breath at rest, a significant decline from their baseline. The patient has experienced unintended weight loss and progressive physical deconditioning over the past six months.
Functional Status: Highly restricted; heavily reliant on spouse for activities of daily living.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver burnout is highly evident; patient is experiencing severe psychological distress related to air hunger.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 28 breaths/min | SpO2: 86% on room air (92% on 3L O2) | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Frail, older adult in marked respiratory distress, tachypneic at rest, utilizing accessory muscles. Pronounced digital clubbing is visible on the hands.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals widespread, fine, bilateral basilar inspiratory crackles resembling the sound of separating Velcro. Elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP) is noted, indicating secondary right heart failure (cor pulmonale). Previous medical records document a usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern with subpleural honeycombing on HRCT.
Environmental Observations: Home oxygen concentrator is running, but the nasal prongs are frequently displaced due to the severe coughing fits.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently on anti-fibrotic therapy (e.g., pirfenidone) and PRN oral morphine for breathlessness.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Infective Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of fever, rigors, or purulent sputum.

Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral symmetrical crackles and equal air entry, lacking acute unilateral hyperresonance or absent breath sounds.

Left-Sided Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The crackles are distinctly "Velcro-like" and dry, characteristic of fibrosis, rather than the wet crackles of pulmonary edema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering an acute exacerbation of symptoms related to advanced Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF). The hallmark Velcro-like crackles, clubbing, and severe resting hypoxemia (SpO2 86% on room air) reflect end-stage fibrotic destruction. The unrelenting dry cough is causing severe physical exhaustion and exacerbating the air hunger, while the elevated JVP indicates right ventricular strain. Delaying intervention until the next in-hours period would leave the patient in agonizing respiratory distress and unmanaged pain. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to titrate palliative medications (opioids for air hunger and cough suppression), optimize oxygen delivery, and establish a clear plan for symptom control.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered an immediate stat dose of oral Morphine solution (2.5mg) to suppress the cough reflex and relieve the sensation of air hunger. Increased home oxygen from 3L to 4L/min via nasal prongs to maintain saturations above 90%.
Positive Behavioral Change: Position a small oscillating fan so that it blows cool air directly across your face; this physical sensation of airflow on the facial nerves directly tricks the brain into feeling less breathless, providing immediate relief without extra medication.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community palliative care team for ongoing symptom management.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow to assess the effectiveness of the opioid dose; discuss advanced care planning if not already in place.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer (call 000) if the patient becomes completely unresponsive, if severe central chest pain develops, or if the breathing distress cannot be managed with the prescribed morphine.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The underlying fibrotic lung disease is progressive and irreversible; treatments are focused entirely on maximizing comfort and reducing the sensation of breathlessness.
Clinical Course: The morphine is not intended to cure the lungs, but it is highly effective at stopping the painful cough and calming the terrifying feeling of suffocation.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call for help immediately if the coughing fits cause you to pass out, or if the small doses of liquid morphine fail to provide relief after 45 minutes.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your stiff lungs are triggering a constant, useless cough reflex and sending panic signals to your brain; the small dose of morphine safely dampens these nerve signals, stopping the cough and allowing you to feel comfortable.
Actionable Steps: Take the liquid morphine exactly when you feel the severe coughing fit starting, rather than waiting until you are exhausted; staying ahead of the symptom prevents the panic cycle and requires less medication overall.

